{"name":"Balanced Beige","hex":"#c0b2a2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"balanced","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of balance"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing elements in appropriate proportion; proportionately weighted on all dimensions and therefore unlikely to tip over."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/balanced"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Balanced line","blocks":[{"text":"In telecommunications and professional audio, a balanced line or balanced signal pair is an electrical circuit consisting of two conductors of the same type, both of which have equal impedances along their lengths, to ground, and to other circuits. The primary advantage of the balanced line format is good rejection of common-mode noise and interference when fed to a differential device such as a transformer or differential amplifier. The combination of a balanced line and differential signaling in audio applications is balanced audio and is commonly used in sound reinforcement and sound recording and reproduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Noise_rejection_on_balanced_line.svg/330px-Noise_rejection_on_balanced_line.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balanced_line","wikidata":"Q2708162"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Klaus-J%C3%BCrgen_Bauer.jpg/500px-Klaus-J%C3%BCrgen_Bauer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Klaus-J%C3%BCrgen_Bauer.jpg/500px-Klaus-J%C3%BCrgen_Bauer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKlaus-J%C3%BCrgen_Bauer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg/500px-1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg/1280px-1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg/500px-1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1981_AMC_Concord_4-door_beige_PAfr.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Coloured_beige_vinyl_%28Funkadelic_-_Maggot_Brain_-_reissue_code_HIQLP_020%29.jpg/500px-Coloured_beige_vinyl_%28Funkadelic_-_Maggot_Brain_-_reissue_code_HIQLP_020%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_